1) DNS Process when you type google.com in browser:
---------------------------------------------------
    User enter the website name:
    ----------------------------
    - when a user enters a website name such as www.google.com into the browser and press enter,
    - the browser first needs to determine the IP address associated with that domain name,
    "since computers communicate using IP address rather than domain names,
    the DNS resolution process begins"

    Browser checks its DNS cache:
    -----------------------------
    - first, the browser will check its own DNS cache to determine whether it has already
      resolved the domain name recently
    - if the browser finds a valid entry in its cache, it will immediately use the cached IP
      address without contacting the DNS server
      ex: www.google.com -> 142.25.xx.xx.xxx

    Operating System checks DNS cache:
    ----------------------------------
    - if the browser does not find the IP address, the request is passed to the operating system,
    - the operating system will check it's local DNS cache to see whether the IP address is already available

    Send DNS Query:
    ---------------
    - if the operating system also does not have the required information, it will send a DNS
      query to the configured DNS server,
    - unlike an ARP request, a DNS query is not a broadcast, it is unicast packet send
      directly to the configured DNS server,
      ex: PC1 -> DNS server (8.8.8.8)

    DNS server checks it's cache:
    -----------------------------
    - when the DNS server receives the request, it will first check whether the requested
      domain name is already present in its own cache
    - if the entry exists, the DNS server immediately returns the corresponding IP address to the client

    Recursive DNS lookup(if needed):
    --------------------------------
    - if the DNS server does not have the answer in its cache, it begins the recursive DNS lookup process
    The DNS server:
    ---------------
    1. Root DNS server
    2. Top-Level Domain (TLD) server (such as .com)
    3. Authoritative DNS server

    Client -> Local DNS -> Root DNS -> .com TLD -> Authoritative DNS -> IP address

    Authoritative DNS server responds:
    ----------------------------------
    - the authoritative DNS server contains the official DNS records for the domain,
    - it returns the IP address associated with www.google.com to the DNS server
    Example: www.google.com -> 142.250.190.36

    DNS server sends the replay:
    ----------------------------
    - after receiving the IP address, the DNS sends a DNS response back to the client
    - the DNS server also stores this information in its cache so that future requests can be answered more quickly

    Client stores the DNS entry:
    ----------------------------
    - the client receives the DNS response and stores the IP address in its local DNS cache
      according to the record's Time To Live (TTL)
    - as long as the TTL has not expired, future requests for the same domain can use the cached
      entry without preforming another DNS lookup

    Communication Starts:
    ---------------------
    - now that the client knows the destination IP address, it can establish communication with the web server
    - if the web server is on the internet, the client will send the packet to its default gateway,
      which forwards it towards the destination
    - if HTTPS is being used, a TCP connection is established first, followed by the TLS handshake,
      and only then does the browser send the HTTP request,

        Example:
        --------
        User types -> Google.com -> Browser Cache -> OS DNS Cache -> DNS Server -> Root DNS -> .com TLD -> Authoritative DNS -> IP Address Returned -> Browser Receives IP -> TCP Connection -> TLS Handshake(HTTPS) -> HTTP Request -> Website Opens

    ** (Interview)
    - when a user enters a website name such as www.google.com,
    - the browser first checks its local DNS cache,
    - if the IP address is not found,
    - the operating system checks its DNS cache,
    - if the entry is still unavailable, the client sends a unicast DNS query to the configured DNS server,
    - the DNS server first checks its own cache, if the record is not available,
    - it performs a recursive lookup by contacting the Root DNS server, then the Top-Level Domain (TLD) server, and finally the Authoritative DNS server,
    - the authoritative server returns the correct IP address to the DNS server,
    - which forwards it to the client and caches it for future use,
    - the client also stores the result in its local cache,
    - once the IP address is known, the client establishes a TCP connection with the destination server and
      then sends the HTTP or HTTPS request to access the website


